# The operator trust root must be the same bytes on every machine that checks it out.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. There was no .gitattributes here, and the default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows
# checks text files out with CRLF. Measured on this machine: the repo-local `core.autocrlf=false` hid the
# problem, while the global setting is `true`, so a plain `git clone` produced a 116-byte
# keys/delegation-operator.pub (3 CR) where the committed blob is 113 bytes. The ledger audit compares
# that file against the local ~/.dsh copy and against the key derived from the private PEM, so the CRLF
# checkout made it abort with "pinned public keys disagree" when the keys were byte-identical apart from
# line endings. The scripts now normalise line endings before comparing, and warn when they have to; this
# prevents the conversion in the first place rather than depending on that tolerance.
#
# `text eol=lf` normalises the stored blob to LF too. The committed blobs are already LF, so this changes
# no existing object -- it only stops a checkout from rewriting them.
*.pub text eol=lf
*.pem text eol=lf
